+## Subtopics
+
+### Illustrative Volume Rendering with Style Transfer
+Focuses on the general application of style transfer to volume data, emphasizing the decoupling of material and appearance. It provides the foundational STF mechanism used here. [Link](11445-illustrative-volume-rendering-with-style-transfer.md)
+
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity**: Linear with respect to the number of samples per ray, which depends on the volume extent and `StepSize`. The style lookup is constant time $O(1)$ per sample.
-*   **Memory Complexity**: Dominated by the `styleTransferTexture` (2D array). With 34 styles and typical resolution (e.g., 256x256 per style), this requires significant VRAM. The volume data and transfer functions are standard.
-*   **Performance**: The method is GPU-intensive due to multiple texture lookups per sample (volume, transfer function, index function, style array). Contour calculations add minor arithmetic overhead.
-*   **Strengths**: Produces high-quality artistic illustrations with consistent style application across surfaces. Contour lines enhance structural perception.
-*   **Limitations**: Requires pre-computed style textures. The curvature approximation for contours is sensitive to `StepSize` and may produce artifacts if sampling is too coarse. The use of forward differences for gradients may reduce normal accuracy compared to central differences.
+*   **Time Complexity:** Linear with respect to the number of samples along the ray. Each sample requires multiple texture lookups (volume, transfer function, index function, style transfer) and gradient computations.
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Dominated by the storage of the 3D volume texture, the 2D array style transfer texture, and the exit points texture. The style transfer texture size is $W \times H \times \text{AVAILABLE\_STYLE\_COUNT}$ [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Performance:** The method is GPU-intensive due to the high number of texture samples per fragment and the use of array textures. The pre-pass for exit points adds overhead but allows for efficient ray marching without front-face intersection tests [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Enables high-quality artistic rendering with minimal changes to the underlying data. The use of lit sphere mapping allows for consistent lighting effects across different styles.
+*   **Limitations:** The quality of the result depends heavily on the design of the style transfer texture and the accuracy of the normal estimation. Contour lines may appear noisy if the step size is too large or the data is sparse [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-This method originates from the FGC_T3 project, an implementation of illustrative volume rendering techniques. It was developed by Jose Villegas and made available on GitHub [S1, unofficial]. The technique combines direct volume ray casting with style transfer mechanisms inspired by image-based rendering and material appearance modeling.
+The method originates from the work on illustrative volume rendering, specifically the FGC_T3 implementation which integrates style transfer functions into the ray casting pipeline. It was developed to allow artists to apply various artistic styles (e.g., pencil sketch, watercolor) to volumetric data based on material properties and surface orientation [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Sources
